Training Callbacks and Early Stopping: To prevent overfitting and ensure optimal convergence, we utilize three specific Keras callbacks: ModelCheckpoint to save the model instance achieving the highest validation accuracy; EarlyStopping to halt training if validation accuracy stagnates for 5 consecutive epochs; and ReduceLROnPlateau to adjust the learning rate as described above.
3.	Data Augmentation:
To enhance model robustness and prevent overfitting, we apply data augmentation exclusively to the training set using the Keras ImageDataGenerator. The augmentation strategy is specifically tailored to the architectural requirements of each model to maximize learning efficiency.
For the BiGRU and BiLSTM-KAN models, we employ a Standard Augmentation protocol. This configuration includes random rotation within ±15◦, a zoom range of ±10%, horizontal flipping with a probability of 0.5, and pixel normalization to the [0, 1] range.
In contrast, the TCN-Residual model utilizes an Enhanced Augmentation strategy to handle its deeper architecture. This approach applies more aggressive transformations, including random rotations up to ±20 degree, width and height shifts of ±15%, zoom variations of ±15%, brightness adjustments in the range [0.8, 1.2], and horizontal flipping.
data leakage occurs between training, validation, and testing phases.
Feature Normalization: All frequency-domain and behavioral features extracted from OpenFace and frequency analysis (FFT, DCT, SRM) are normalized using StandardScaler. The scaler is fitted exclusively on the training data from each fold and then applied to validation and test sets to prevent information leakage. This produces features with zero mean and unit variance, facilitating faster convergence during training.
Sequence Formation: Video frames are organized into fixed-length temporal sequences adapted to the model architecture. We define a sequence length of T =
10 frames for the BiGRU and BiLSTM-KAN models, whereas the TCN-Residual model utilizes a longer context of T = 15 frames. For videos with fewer frames than the sequence length, zero-padding is applied. For longer videos, the first T frames are selected to maintain consistency across the dataset.
HMM Post-Processing: Following model prediction, we apply a Gaussian HMM with 2 hidden states (diagonal covariance, 100 EM iterations) for temporal smoothing. The HMM is fitted on the predicted probabilities and the hidden-state sequence is decoded for post-processing. In our implementation, the state-to-label mapping is determined by majority voting using the fold’s ground-truth labels. This heuristic improves temporal consistency, but it should be interpreted as a post-processing rule rather than an end-to-end learned module.
This comprehensive implementation strategy ensures reproducible results and fair comparison across different model architectures while maintaining rigorous separation between training and evaluation data.

Main results
This section presents the comparative evaluation of our three proposed architectures: TCN-Residual, BiLSTM-KAN, and BiGRU-Attention, on the FaceForensics++ (FF++) and Celeb-DF datasets. All experiments were conducted using a stratified 5-fold cross-validation scheme to assess model generalization.

Table 2 summarizes the averaged performance metrics across all folds for both datasets. We utilize Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, and AUC to provide a comprehensive assessment.


